WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.880
Hi, Mystery Recapped here. Today I am&nbsp;
going to explain an American docudrama film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:15.920
called “Front of the Class.” Spoilers&nbsp;
ahead! Watch out and take care!
&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:22.560
Twelve-year-old Brad Cohen has Tourette syndrome.&nbsp;
Because of the condition, he makes strange noises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:29.200
twitches, and yells randomly. All these actions&nbsp;
are involuntary and Brad has no control over them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.920
But in the small town in Missouri, most people&nbsp;
are unfamiliar with Brad’s unique syndrome. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:39.840
do not believe him when he claims he cannot stop&nbsp;
the tics. Even his father Norman thinks his son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:45.200
is making the noise to trouble his parents. 
Brad’s classmates bully him often and the teachers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:50.080
are of no help. The tics started when he was six&nbsp;
years old, around the time Brad’s parents were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:55.280
getting divorced. Ever since, his mother has&nbsp;
taken him to several doctors, all of whom have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:01.040
associated his tics with depression. That is why&nbsp;
the real cause has never been diagnosed and people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:06.960
continue to believe he is just a spoiled brat.
Brad’s only friend is his younger brother, Jeff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.840
Although they fight and bicker all the time,&nbsp;
Brad feels like Jeff is the only person who gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.880
him. Their father Norman lives in another city and&nbsp;
visits them once in a while. He connects with Jeff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:22.320
but Brad has always been sort of an embarrassment&nbsp;
for him. He often scolds the kid to stop yelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.760
and moving so much. His father's distrust&nbsp;
in him makes Brad feel like a failure. 
&nbsp;

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:32.320
Meanwhile, Brad’s mother Ellen has always been&nbsp;
supportive of her son. She believes him when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.640
he says the tics are not something he can&nbsp;
control. Ellen is troubled by her friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:42.400
and relatives suggesting different absurd methods&nbsp;
to discipline her kid. Although she trusts Brad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:47.680
being a single mother with two kids, one of&nbsp;
whom cannot stop yelling, is tiring for her.
&nbsp;

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.640
One day, she takes Brad to a new therapist in the&nbsp;
town. The man asks him about his parents’ divorce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:57.040
and its effect on his life. Brad simply tells&nbsp;
him that he doesn’t remember much about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:02.000
incident and it doesn’t bother him at all.&nbsp;
The doctor refuses to believe it and claims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.960
that Brad is in denial of the effect, which&nbsp;
might be causing the tics. He is yet again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.840
misdiagnosed and told that the tics will&nbsp;
go away once he disciplines himself.
&nbsp;

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:17.360
The only time Norman feels proud of being Brad’s&nbsp;
father is when Brad plays baseball. In the field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:22.240
everyone makes noises and moves, which makes&nbsp;
Brad feel like it is the only place he fits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:27.680
However, after every match, Norman brings his&nbsp;
sons home immediately to not embarrass himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.320
in front of the other fathers. Sometimes, the&nbsp;
constant moving and twitching make Norman lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:37.040
his temper. He often lashes out at Brad,&nbsp;
unaware that he cannot stop them. 
&nbsp;

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:42.240
Brad usually gets good grades and is excellent&nbsp;
at studying. However, trying to concentrate on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.520
exams brings out the worst in him. The stress&nbsp;
makes his tics frequent and uncontrollable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.600
One day, his science teacher brings him to&nbsp;
the principal’s office for the hundredth time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.480
for the same reason. The principal calls his&nbsp;
mother and agrees to give Bard the last chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.760
to stop the mischievous behavior. Ellen insists&nbsp;
that her son cannot help it but the principal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.400
in turn, calls her a bad parent for making&nbsp;
excuses for Brad’s bad behavior. 
&nbsp;

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:10.960
Having had enough of everyone blaming her&nbsp;
son, Ellen takes it upon herself to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:15.760
out the real cause of his symptoms. She does&nbsp;
extensive research on conditions that cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:20.000
people to yell and twitch. In the end,&nbsp;
she comes across Tourettes syndrome.
&nbsp;

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.400
Brad finally has a way to prove that&nbsp;
he is not lying or being mischievous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.520
His therapist is also told about the discovery&nbsp;
and he praises Ellen for her great work. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:35.040
the joy of finding out the problem vanishes when&nbsp;
Ellen realizes that the condition has no cure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.480
Her son will have to learn to live&nbsp;
with the noises, the twitches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:42.640
and the attention that comes with it.
Still, the saddest person to find out about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:47.680
Brad’s condition is his father Norman. He is&nbsp;
guilt-ridden that he never believed his son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.840
but more than that, he is distressed that&nbsp;
the kid might not have a good future. 
&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.520
Although the cure is impossible, Ellen is&nbsp;
determined to teach her kid to learn to live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:00.000
with Tourettes. One day, she takes him to a&nbsp;
support group for people with the same syndrome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.480
as him. Meeting them was supposed to make Brad&nbsp;
feel better, but it makes him feel quite the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.760
opposite. He feels like he is seeing himself from&nbsp;
other people’s eyes, and he doesn’t like the view.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:14.000
Moreover, he finds out that everyone in&nbsp;
the room has given up on their lives. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.000
kids are homeschooled while the adults&nbsp;
get work-from-home-type jobs. 
&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:23.200
The experience is awful for Brad but it&nbsp;
is also the defining moment in his life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.200
Starting that day, he makes it his mission&nbsp;
to not be like the people from the support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:32.320
group. He promises to be his own person and&nbsp;
never let the disability hold him back.
&nbsp;

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:37.120
Even after he changes schools in seventh grade,&nbsp;
not much progress is seen in how people treat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:42.160
him. The teachers always send him to the principal&nbsp;
and the classmates make fun of him. That is until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:47.520
the principal finds out about Brad’s condition and&nbsp;
invites him to the stage during a school program.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.640
Brad is asked to explain his syndrome to the&nbsp;
audience and explain what they can do to help.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.920
After finding out that he actually&nbsp;
cannot help but make the noises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:01.680
people start being more sympathetic to him. 
His life is never the same again. The principal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:06.640
becomes that one teacher who helps him with&nbsp;
his biggest problem with ease and simplicity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:11.360
This makes Brad realize that he also wants&nbsp;
to be a teacher like him in the future.
&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:17.680
Time flies by and Brad soon grows into a handsome&nbsp;
young man with a bachelor's diploma in education.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:22.720
He moves to Atlanta and gets an apartment with&nbsp;
a roommate, hoping to land a teaching job.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:26.800
The officer at the state education&nbsp;
board is impressed by his qualification.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:31.920
However, she is skeptical if teaching would be a&nbsp;
suitable profession for someone with Tourettes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:36.960
Not just her, but most people Brad knows,&nbsp;
think the same about his choice of profession.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:42.240
He understands their concerns to some level but he&nbsp;
knows in his heart that he was born to teach. 
&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.800
The very next day, he goes to his first&nbsp;
job interview. His tics visibly bother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:51.280
the interviewer but he is not ashamed or&nbsp;
uncomfortable to explain his condition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:56.160
He also makes sure to assure the man that his&nbsp;
tics are an open topic for the students to discuss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:01.120
and it won’t harm their studies in any way.&nbsp;
But the interviewer doesn’t seem convinced.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:04.720
In the end, he is waitlisted. 
The same thing happens at his next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:09.600
interview and the one after that. He uses several&nbsp;
different methods, like talking about his tics for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:14.720
the entire interview or not acknowledging it&nbsp;
at all. But none of them land him a job. 
&nbsp;

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:19.600
To take his mind off things, his roommate sets&nbsp;
him up on a blind date. He meets a girl at a bar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:24.240
and is instantly attracted to her. The only&nbsp;
problem arises when people around them stare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:29.280
at them because of Brad’s tics. By the end of&nbsp;
the night, his date is visibly uncomfortable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.240
be seen with him. It turns out that Brad has&nbsp;
never landed a second date with any woman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:38.800
He still hopes that one day, he will find&nbsp;
someone who can see him past his tics.
&nbsp;

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:42.960
Brad’s father owns a construction business in&nbsp;
Atlanta and is ready to help him with money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:47.920
until he gets a job. But Brad doesn’t want to&nbsp;
accept favors from Norman. The man still thinks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:52.400
that Brad cannot survive as a teacher and&nbsp;
voices his opinion every chance he gets.
&nbsp;

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:57.440
After twenty-five failed attempts at job&nbsp;
interviews, Brad has a mental breakdown in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:03.200
his car. He feels hopeless and broken. But after&nbsp;
talking to his mother over the phone, he realizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:07.360
that the tics aren’t the reason for his failure,&nbsp;
he just hasn’t found the right school. 
&nbsp;

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:11.520
For the twenty-sixth interview, he goes&nbsp;
to an elementary school. The principal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:16.080
and the vice-principal are already well informed&nbsp;
about Tourettes syndrome and do not make Brad feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:20.560
uncomfortable by treating him differently.&nbsp;
The interview goes on for two hours and is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:25.920
the best one he has ever had. By now, Brad&nbsp;
has learned to not have high hopes, still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.880
he cannot help but wish to get the job.
To his surprise, the next day, he is called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:36.080
to teach second graders. He is over the moon at&nbsp;
the news. Although he only has two days to prepare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:41.040
for the class, he cannot care less about the&nbsp;
time and is excited to finally get to teach. 
&nbsp;

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:45.840
His fellow teachers welcome him and help him set&nbsp;
up the classroom. For the first time in his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:51.120
Brad finds people who do not judge him for his&nbsp;
tics. At night, he calls his mother who pressures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:56.640
him into dating a woman. Brad takes her advice&nbsp;
and meets a tinder date named Nancy the next day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:01.440
The two immediately hit it off but Bard feels&nbsp;
like he will be ignored after the first date&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:06.160
like he always has. Following the fun date,&nbsp;
they separate with a simple goodbye. 
&nbsp;

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:10.400
Later, Brad gets an email from her&nbsp;
praising his taste in music. He gets the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:15.920
hint and invites Nancy to a second date. 
The next day is his first day as a teacher.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:20.080
Initially, the children laugh at his tics.&nbsp;
But then, he explains to them what Tourettes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:24.880
syndrome is and what it does to people. Even&nbsp;
the second graders seem to be understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:29.360
after finding out Brad cannot control the&nbsp;
noises. They ask him several questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:33.920
all of which he answers happily. By the&nbsp;
end of the day, the kids love him.
&nbsp;

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:38.240
But the same cannot be said for the parents.&nbsp;
When they come to pick their children up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:42.320
they seem to not like the new teacher.&nbsp;
Brad senses this but is confident that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:47.680
they will like him after knowing him better. 
Meanwhile, he continues dating Nancy and starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:52.960
falling in love for the first time. She genuinely&nbsp;
doesn’t seem to care about his tics and likes him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:57.920
for his personality. In school, Brad bonds with&nbsp;
a cheerful little girl named Heather. She is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:02.880
smartest one in the class but it is her kindness&nbsp;
that catches his attention. When the other kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:07.920
make fun of Brad, she shuts them down. Some&nbsp;
parents request their kids to not be in Brad’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:13.040
class because of his disability. The school allows&nbsp;
it but Brad is asked to keep his head up because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:18.400
the parents are the hardest part of the job. 
One afternoon after school, Heather comments that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:24.320
she is about to lose her hair. Her mother confirms&nbsp;
that the little girl has cancer and is undergoing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:31.440
chemo. Still, the chances of her survival are&nbsp;
very low. The news saddens Brad to his core. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:36.400
mother also adds that Heather cannot wait to come&nbsp;
to school nowadays; he is the only person keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:42.640
her happy during the last few months of her life.&nbsp;
The news only makes Brad feel bittersweet.
&nbsp;

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:47.600
A few weeks later, Brad goes to take a test for&nbsp;
the master's degree but isn’t given a private room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:52.880
as promised. He talks to the administration but&nbsp;
they refuse to help him. When he starts to panic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:57.440
Norman comes to his aid and threatens to sue&nbsp;
the administration. They eventually agree to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:02.640
let him take the test in private. The incident&nbsp;
is a bonding moment for the father and son.
&nbsp;

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:06.800
In the following scene, we see Nancy and Brad&nbsp;
go to his mother’s house for thanksgiving.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:12.240
Seeing her socialize with her family, Brad cannot&nbsp;
help but get emotional. He pulls Nancy aside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:18.160
and confesses his love for the first time. 
Eventually, Heather stops coming to school. Her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:23.760
classmates make letters and postcards wishing her&nbsp;
good health. But one day, they get the news they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:29.040
had been dreading to hear. At Heather’s funeral,&nbsp;
Brad refrains himself from going inside, thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:34.000
that he will disrupt the ceremony. But her mother&nbsp;
thanks him for being her daughter’s sunshine in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:39.040
her darkest days and invites him in. 
A few months later, Brad’s life is running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:44.240
smoothly. He has a loving girlfriend, a good&nbsp;
relationship with his father, and a job that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:50.160
loves. Then, one day, he is called to a school&nbsp;
meeting and informed that he is being awarded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:55.040
Sallie Mae’s first teacher of the year.
On the night of the event, Brad gives a heartfelt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.040 --> 00:11:00.640
speech that everyone loves. The movie ends with&nbsp;
a message that we should never let any obstacle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:05.200
stop us from pursuing our dreams.
Subscribe for more videos like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:11.040
turn on the notification, and leave a like to help&nbsp;
the channel out. Thanks for watching.

